## 4. Generating a single node on demand

When the learner clicks a link to a missing id, you'll be given the id, the link label, the
parent node and the depth. Reply with **just that one node** — the same shape as an entry of
`nodes`, without the id:

```json
{ "range": "11:20-11:29", "title": "String concatenation", "text": "`+` between a string and a number turns the number into text and joins them: `\"i = \" + 1` is `\"i = 1\"`." }
```

It can link to further missing ids in the same way. When answering a learner's free-text question
about a node, reply with a node in the same shape.

---

## 5. Checklist before saving

- [ ] Valid JSON: double quotes, no trailing commas, no comments.
- [ ] There is a `nodes` object and every node has a string `range`.
- [ ] `root` names a node that exists.
- [ ] Every range is inside the file, and you re-checked line numbers against the file.
- [ ] Every `file` path is project-relative and exists.
- [ ] Every `next` points at a node that exists.
- [ ] Every `#id` link either exists or is deliberately left for on-demand generation.
- [ ] Questions have an `answer`, and the answer isn't given away in `text` or `hints`.
- [ ] Markdown inside JSON strings is escaped: `\"` for quotes, `\n` for new lines.

If the plugin rejects the file, the error names the node and the field at fault (e.g.
`Node 'loop' needs a "range"`). Fix that field and save again.

---

## 6. Full example

This annotates a simple `Main.java` (a `main()` method on lines 3–13 that prints a greeting on
line 6 and loops `i` from 1 to 5 on lines 8–12, printing it on line 11):

```json
{
  "file": "Main.java",
  "root": "main",
  "theme": { "palette": ["yellow", "blue", "green", "pink"], "maxWidth": 480 },
  "nodes": {
    "main": {
      "range": "3-13",
      "title": "The main method",
      "kind": "info",
      "text": "This is the program's **entry point**. It:\n\n1. [prints a greeting](#greet)\n2. [loops five times](#loop)\n\nTake the [guided tour](#tour1) for a step-by-step walkthrough.",
      "style": { "highlightStyle": "bar" }
    },
    "greet": {
      "range": "6",
      "title": "Printing a greeting",
      "text": "`IO.println` prints a line to the console. The text is [this string literal](6:30-6:49)."
    },
    "loop": {
      "range": "8-12",
      "title": "A `for` loop",
      "kind": "tip",
      "text": "Counts `i` from 1 to 5. The outline shows where `i` is used.\n\nEach pass [prints the counter](#print).",
      "also": ["11"]
    },
    "print": {
      "range": "11",
      "kind": "question",
      "title": "Check your understanding",
      "text": "What does this line print on the **third** pass of the loop?\n\nIt uses [string concatenation](#concat).",
      "hints": ["`i` starts at 1 and goes up by one each pass.", "`\"i = \" + i` joins the text and the number."],
      "answer": "`i = 3`"
    },
    "tour1": {
      "range": "3",
      "title": "Tour 1/3: the signature",
      "kind": "example",
      "text": "`void main()` declares a method with no return value. Java runs it first.",
      "next": "tour2"
    },
    "tour2": {
      "range": "6",
      "title": "Tour 2/3: output",
      "kind": "example",
      "text": "Prints `Hello and welcome!`.",
      "next": "tour3"
    },
    "tour3": {
      "range": "8-12",
      "title": "Tour 3/3: the loop",
      "kind": "example",
      "text": "Prints `i = 1` to `i = 5`. That's the end of the tour!"
    }
  }
}
```

Here `#concat` is left undefined on purpose: it is generated when the learner clicks it.

